cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this dataset or the measurement protocol in your work, please cite it as below."
title: "Language Advantage Is a Function of the Executor: criteria self-audit and undecidability audit in cross-language code generation by LLM coders (timetable-ab)"
abstract: >-
  Original proposition: when the executor is an AI large language model, does Lisp
  have a measurable language advantage? Eleven levers were pre-registered and tested
  across seven languages (Common Lisp, JavaScript, Java, Julia, Rust, Python, C++).
  What this release defends as re-checkable is deliberately narrower: two languages,
  a 15-operator specification, 36 invariants, plus 24 cells generated with archived
  prompts and frozen spec/criteria hashes; a second layer of 13 independent
  implementations under 80 invariants is kept as a prior account. Runs that did not
  archive their task descriptions are reported as observations and never as evidence.
  Results are reported three ways: three claims established, three refuted, and six
  undecidable. The undecidable class is the point of this release: three conditions
  are missing (no weak-executor channel, no exclusive machine or clock alignment, a
  single model family), two further items are incomplete (silent-failure coverage:
  12 mutants audited, 8 killed, 4 survivors all classified, which is not the same as
  "no blind spots"; scale limited to 30 ops). See CONDITIONS.md. The dataset does not
  read "not detected" as "not present", does not extrapolate beyond the measured
  scale, and does not delete data.
